  "description": "Build-first origination gate, reworked to @pipavlo82's three-fact contract: content identity / existence witness / precedence. Timestamp authority is NOT uniform across anchor kinds. An on-chain tx/deploy carries its own witness (the block timestamp is consensus-set); a bare git commit does not (author/committer dates are backdatable fields inside the object). So a git anchor PASSES only with a SEPARATE independent witness (on-chain commitment of the commit hash, transparency-log entry, or forge event); with none it is UNVERIFIABLE:no_publication_witness, never PASS. `record.anchor` carries NO self-reported time. `resolution` is a MODEL INPUT (companion resolve_anchor.py produces the real one): content in {confirmed, not_found, unavailable_pruned, unavailable_rpc, unavailable_source}; witness_declared bool; witnessed_ts the INDEPENDENTLY-witnessed time (or null). Precedence compares witnessed_ts, never a declared value. Positive controls: the two on-chain anchors (8299, 8373) PASS on their consensus witness; the bare-commit 8309 is UNVERIFIABLE until witnessed, and PASSES only when accompanied by an on-chain commitment witness. The witness `tx` values in the git-witnessed cases (0x0000…, 0x1111…, 0x2222…) are OPAQUE MODEL FIXTURES — no such on-chain commitment exists yet for 8309; the resolution's witnessed_ts is the model input (exactly as pq-key-binding.v1/manifest models membership), so no real witness is claimed here. The two on-chain positive controls, by contrast, carry REAL Base Sepolia txs whose block timestamps resolve live. Proves temporal witness + existence only, never semantic origination.",
